-Batching a quantized language model can change its logits even when token positions and causal masks remain unchanged. We present reference-derived phase plans that preserve a fixed deployment's numerical behavior while executing known queries in parallel. A plan combines physical KV-prefix requirements, qualified arithmetic recipes and logical output ownership on one resident target state. Whole-graph and local-attention lowerings retain different amounts of outer parallelism. Independent-channel packing preserves four-column CUDA Q8 reductions and extends to scalar-column Q4\_K/Q6\_K arithmetic. Native studies cover Qwen, Llama and SmolLM2, Windows and Linux, and CUDA and Vulkan. On Linux/A5000, compatible speculative execution reduces resident-service time by 17.21\%, 25.54\% and 11.52\% relative to the fastest measured fixed target-only control for Qwen3-8B, Qwen3-14B and Llama-8B, respectively. Balanced factorial studies separate packing from local-cut effects. In a 32B mixed-quantization deployment, channel grouping reduces ordinary service time by 5.61\% relative to graph-level scalar-column execution. Equal-work eight-query verification is also faster than two four-query calls in the tested RTX cells. The results establish an execution method that composes reference arithmetic with physical state, and show how its parallelism translates into complete-request value.
+Batching a quantized language model can change its numerical output even when token positions and causal masks stay the same. We introduce reference-derived phase plans for parallel inference that retains an established deployment's numerical behavior. A plan binds the prompt producer, physical KV range, arithmetic recipe and ownership of outputs and state. It places cuts around whole graphs or within attention, while packing independent arithmetic groups into wider operations. Implementations on CUDA and Vulkan cover Qwen, Llama and SmolLM2, including a mixed-Q4 32B model. On Linux/A5000, compatible speculative applications reduce resident-service time by 17.21\%, 25.54\% and 11.52\% against the fastest measured fixed target-only control for Qwen3-8B, Qwen3-14B and Llama-8B. Separate balanced experiments attribute 0.80\%--1.64\% to Q8 projection packing; local cuts contribute smaller, geometry-dependent gains. For 32B mixed quantization, independent-channel grouping reduces ordinary service time by 5.61\% relative to a graph-level scalar-column loop. Eight-query execution also lowers aggregate cost at equal work, while longer live proposals add enough work to favor the shorter configuration. Phase plans thus preserve reference-sensitive computation while exposing parallel work, with complete-request costs guiding the choice of execution strategy.
